en:

'<p class="l-text"><strong>GroupBy is the single most powerful concept in Pandas.</strong> It lets you split your data into groups, apply a function to each group independently, and combine the results back into a single structure. Whether you are computing per-category statistics, normalizing features within groups, or engineering features for ML -- <code>groupby()</code> is at the core of it all.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text">This lesson covers the full groupby ecosystem: <strong>aggregation</strong> (reducing groups to scalars), <strong>transformation</strong> (producing same-shape output), <strong>filtering</strong> (keeping/dropping entire groups), <strong>pivot tables</strong>, <strong>cross tabulations</strong>, and <strong>window functions</strong> for rolling statistics.</p>'
+ '<div class="lesson-outcomes" style="background:rgba(200,169,110,0.06);border-left:3px solid #c8a96e;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.08em;color:#c8a96e;margin-bottom:0.6rem">📍 WHAT YOU\'LL LEARN</div><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;line-height:1.65;font-size:0.93rem;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.92)"><li>Group rows by one or many columns with DataFrame.groupby()</li><li>Aggregate groups with sum, mean, count and custom functions via .agg()</li><li>Apply multiple aggregations to multiple columns in a single call</li><li>Use .transform() to broadcast group-level statistics back onto rows</li><li>Filter groups with .filter() based on group-wide predicates</li><li>Combine groupby with sort, rank and head to get top-N per group</li></ul></div>'


/* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: Split-Apply-Combine
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Split-Apply-Combine</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The paradigm:</strong> Every <code>groupby()</code> operation follows the same three-step pattern: (1) <strong>Split</strong> the DataFrame into groups based on one or more keys, (2) <strong>Apply</strong> a function to each group independently, (3) <strong>Combine</strong> the results back into a single DataFrame or Series. This is called the <em>Split-Apply-Combine</em> strategy.</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Split</div><div class="card-body"><code>df.groupby("col")</code> creates a GroupBy object. No computation happens yet -- it is <strong>lazy</strong>. It just stores how to split the data by unique values in the column.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Apply</div><div class="card-body">Call an aggregation (<code>.mean()</code>), transformation (<code>.transform()</code>), or filter (<code>.filter()</code>) on the GroupBy object. The function runs on each group independently.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Combine</div><div class="card-body">Pandas automatically reassembles the results. Aggregations produce a smaller DataFrame (one row per group). Transformations return the same shape as the input.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Create the GroupBy Object</div><div class="step-detail"><code>grouped = df.groupby("field")</code> -- This returns a <strong>DataFrameGroupBy</strong> object, not a DataFrame. Think of it as a collection of sub-DataFrames, one per unique value of "field". Nothing is computed yet.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Inspect the Groups</div><div class="step-detail"><code>grouped.groups</code> returns a dict mapping group keys to row indices. <code>grouped.ngroups</code> tells you how many groups exist. <code>grouped.get_group("NLP")</code> returns the sub-DataFrame for a specific group.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Apply a Function</div><div class="step-detail">Call <code>.mean()</code>, <code>.sum()</code>, <code>.agg()</code>, <code>.transform()</code>, or <code>.filter()</code>. Each function operates on every group independently, then Pandas combines the results automatically.</div></div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Sample dataset: students with fields and scores</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>:  [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>, <span class="str">"Frank"</span>, <span class="str">"Grace"</span>, <span class="str">"Hank"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">85</span>, <span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">76</span>, <span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">79</span>, <span class="num">91</span>, <span class="num">82</span>],\n    <span class="str">"hours"</span>: [<span class="num">40</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">38</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">45</span>, <span class="num">32</span>, <span class="num">42</span>, <span class="num">28</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Step 1: Create GroupBy object (lazy -- no computation)</span>\ngrouped = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>(grouped))   <span class="cm"># DataFrameGroupBy</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Inspect groups</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(grouped.ngroups)          <span class="cm"># 3 (CV, DL, NLP)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(grouped.groups)           <span class="cm"># {\'CV\': [1,3,7], \'DL\': [4,6], \'NLP\': [0,2,5]}</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(grouped.<span class="fn">size</span>())           <span class="cm"># CV: 3, DL: 2, NLP: 3</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Step 2: Get a specific group</span>\nnlp_group = grouped.<span class="fn">get_group</span>(<span class="str">"NLP"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(nlp_group)\n<span class="cm">#     name field  score  hours</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  Alice   NLP     92     40</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2  Carol   NLP     88     38</span>\n<span class="cm"># 5  Frank   NLP     79     32</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Step 3: Apply aggregation</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(grouped[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>())\n<span class="cm"># field</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV     81.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL     93.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP    86.333...</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Grouping by multiple columns</strong> creates a MultiIndex in the result:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Multi-column groupby</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"level"</span>] = [<span class="str">"grad"</span>, <span class="str">"undergrad"</span>, <span class="str">"grad"</span>, <span class="str">"undergrad"</span>,\n               <span class="str">"grad"</span>, <span class="str">"undergrad"</span>, <span class="str">"grad"</span>, <span class="str">"undergrad"</span>]\n\nmulti = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>([<span class="str">"field"</span>, <span class="str">"level"</span>])[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>()\n<span class="fn">print</span>(multi)\n<span class="cm"># field  level</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV     undergrad    81.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL     grad         93.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP    grad         90.0</span>\n<span class="cm">#        undergrad    79.0</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Iterate over groups</span>\n<span class="kw">for</span> name, group <span class="kw">in</span> df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="kw">f</span><span class="str">"--- {name} (n={len(group)}) ---"</span>)\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(group[[<span class="str">"name"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>]])</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Split-Apply-Combine Visualization EN --- */
+ '<div id="plot-sac-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ 'var fields=["NLP","NLP","NLP","CV","CV","CV","DL","DL"];'
