+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> Each bar represents a student\'s score, colored by their field. The dashed horizontal lines show the group mean after applying <code>.mean()</code>. This is the Split-Apply-Combine pattern in action: split by field, apply mean, combine the results.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body">The GroupBy object is <strong>lazy</strong> -- creating it costs almost nothing. The computation only happens when you call an aggregation or transformation. This means you can safely write <code>grouped = df.groupby("col")</code> and reuse it multiple times without performance penalty. Pandas internally stores a mapping of group keys to row indices, not copies of the data.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: Aggregation
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Aggregation</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Aggregation reduces each group to a single value.</strong> The <code>.agg()</code> method is the most flexible aggregation tool -- it lets you apply one or more functions, use custom functions, and rename the output columns all in a single call.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Built-in aggregation functions</strong> -- these are optimized C-level implementations that are much faster than custom Python functions:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">sum / mean</div><div class="card-body"><code>.sum()</code> adds all values. <code>.mean()</code> computes the arithmetic mean. Both skip NaN by default (<code>skipna=True</code>).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">count / size</div><div class="card-body"><code>.count()</code> counts non-NaN values per column. <code>.size()</code> counts all rows including NaN. Use <code>size</code> for total group sizes.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">min / max</div><div class="card-body"><code>.min()</code> and <code>.max()</code> return extremes. Works on numbers, strings (alphabetical), and dates.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">std / var</div><div class="card-body"><code>.std()</code> standard deviation, <code>.var()</code> variance. Default is <code>ddof=1</code> (sample, not population). Change with <code>.std(ddof=0)</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">first / last</div><div class="card-body"><code>.first()</code> returns the first non-NaN value per group. <code>.last()</code> returns the last. Useful for time series data.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">median / quantile</div><div class="card-body"><code>.median()</code> returns the 50th percentile. <code>.quantile(0.75)</code> returns any percentile you need.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>:  [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>, <span class="str">"Frank"</span>, <span class="str">"Grace"</span>, <span class="str">"Hank"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">85</span>, <span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">76</span>, <span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">79</span>, <span class="num">91</span>, <span class="num">82</span>],\n    <span class="str">"hours"</span>: [<span class="num">40</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">38</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">45</span>, <span class="num">32</span>, <span class="num">42</span>, <span class="num">28</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Single aggregation -- shortcut methods</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>())\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">std</span>())\n\n<span class="cm"># Multiple aggregations with .agg()</span>\nresult = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">agg</span>([<span class="str">"mean"</span>, <span class="str">"std"</span>, <span class="str">"min"</span>, <span class="str">"max"</span>, <span class="str">"count"</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n<span class="cm">#         mean       std  min  max  count</span>\n<span class="cm"># field</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV     81.00  4.582...   76   85      3</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL     93.00  2.828...   91   95      2</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP    86.33  6.658...   79   92      3</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Different aggregations per column</span>\nresult = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>({\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="str">"mean"</span>, <span class="str">"std"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"hours"</span>: [<span class="str">"sum"</span>, <span class="str">"mean"</span>]\n})\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n<span class="cm">#        score              hours</span>\n<span class="cm">#         mean       std     sum  mean</span>\n<span class="cm"># field</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV     81.00  4.582...      93  31.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL     93.00  2.828...      87  43.5</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP    86.33  6.658...     110  36.7</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Named aggregation</strong> -- the modern way to get clean, flat column names:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Named aggregation (Pandas 0.25+) -- clean column names!</span>\nresult = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>(\n    avg_score  = pd.<span class="fn">NamedAgg</span>(column=<span class="str">"score"</span>, aggfunc=<span class="str">"mean"</span>),\n    max_score  = pd.<span class="fn">NamedAgg</span>(column=<span class="str">"score"</span>, aggfunc=<span class="str">"max"</span>),\n    total_hrs  = pd.<span class="fn">NamedAgg</span>(column=<span class="str">"hours"</span>, aggfunc=<span class="str">"sum"</span>),\n    n_students = pd.<span class="fn">NamedAgg</span>(column=<span class="str">"score"</span>, aggfunc=<span class="str">"count"</span>)\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n<span class="cm">#        avg_score  max_score  total_hrs  n_students</span>\n<span class="cm"># field</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV         81.00         85         93           3</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL         93.00         95         87           2</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP        86.33         92        110           3</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Shorthand (same result, less verbose)</span>\nresult = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>(\n    avg_score  = (<span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"mean"</span>),\n    max_score  = (<span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"max"</span>),\n    total_hrs  = (<span class="str">"hours"</span>, <span class="str">"sum"</span>),\n    n_students = (<span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"count"</span>)\n)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Custom aggregation functions</strong> -- for anything the built-ins do not cover:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Custom function receives a Series (one group at a time)</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">score_range</span>(s):\n    <span class="kw">return</span> s.<span class="fn">max</span>() - s.<span class="fn">min</span>()\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">coeff_of_variation</span>(s):\n    <span class="kw">return</span> s.<span class="fn">std</span>() / s.<span class="fn">mean</span>() * <span class="num">100</span>  <span class="cm"># percentage</span>\n\nresult = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">agg</span>(\n    [<span class="str">"mean"</span>, score_range, coeff_of_variation]\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n<span class="cm">#         mean  score_range  coeff_of_variation</span>\n<span class="cm"># field</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV     81.00           9               5.66</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL     93.00           4               3.04</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP    86.33          13               7.71</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Lambda functions (quick and dirty)</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">agg</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x.<span class="fn">quantile</span>(<span class="num">0.75</span>) - x.<span class="fn">quantile</span>(<span class="num">0.25</span>)  <span class="cm"># IQR</span>\n)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Performance tip:</strong> Built-in aggregations (<code>"mean"</code>, <code>"sum"</code>, etc.) are implemented in C/Cython and are 10-100x faster than custom Python functions. Always check if a built-in exists before writing a custom function. Use <code>.describe()</code> to get summary statistics for all groups in one call.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: Transformation & Filtering
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Transformation & Filtering</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Transform returns same-shape output.</strong> While <code>.agg()</code> reduces each group to one row, <code>.transform()</code> returns a value for every row in the original DataFrame. This is essential for operations like <strong>z-score normalization within groups</strong>, <strong>filling NaN with group means</strong>, or <strong>computing percentages within groups</strong>.</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">agg() -- Reduces</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Output has one row per group</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Input: 8 rows, 3 groups &rarr; Output: 3 rows</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Use for: summary statistics, reporting</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Example: mean score per field</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">transform() -- Same Shape</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Output has same rows as input</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Input: 8 rows &rarr; Output: 8 rows</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Use for: normalization, feature engineering</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Example: z-score within each field</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>:  [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>, <span class="str">"Frank"</span>, <span class="str">"Grace"</span>, <span class="str">"Hank"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"DL"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">85</span>, <span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">76</span>, <span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">79</span>, <span class="num">91</span>, <span class="num">82</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Z-score normalization within each group</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"z_score"</span>] = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">transform</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> x: (x - x.<span class="fn">mean</span>()) / x.<span class="fn">std</span>()\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[[<span class="str">"name"</span>, <span class="str">"field"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"z_score"</span>]])\n<span class="cm">#     name field  score   z_score</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  Alice   NLP     92    0.852</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1    Bob    CV     85    0.873</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2  Carol   NLP     88    0.252</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3   Dave    CV     76   -1.091</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4    Eve    DL     95    0.707</span>\n<span class="cm"># 5  Frank   NLP     79   -1.104</span>\n<span class="cm"># 6  Grace    DL     91   -0.707</span>\n<span class="cm"># 7   Hank    CV     82    0.218</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Broadcast group mean back to each row</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"group_mean"</span>] = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">transform</span>(<span class="str">"mean"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Deviation from group mean</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"dev_from_mean"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"score"</span>] - df[<span class="str">"group_mean"</span>]\n\n<span class="cm"># Percentage of group total</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"pct_of_group"</span>] = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">transform</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x / x.<span class="fn">sum</span>() * <span class="num">100</span>\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># Fill NaN with group mean (very common!)</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"filled"</span>] = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">transform</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x.<span class="fn">fillna</span>(x.<span class="fn">mean</span>())\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># Rank within each group</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"group_rank"</span>] = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>)[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">rank</span>(ascending=<span class="kw">False</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Group filtering</strong> -- keep or drop entire groups based on a condition:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># filter() keeps groups where the function returns True</span>\n<span class="cm"># Keep only fields with average score > 85</span>\nhigh_avg = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>).<span class="fn">filter</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> g: g[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>() > <span class="num">85</span>\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(high_avg)\n<span class="cm"># Only NLP (86.3) and DL (93.0) groups remain</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV (81.0) is dropped entirely</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Keep only groups with at least 3 members</span>\nlarge_groups = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>).<span class="fn">filter</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> g: <span class="fn">len</span>(g) >= <span class="num">3</span>\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># Keep groups where all scores are above a threshold</span>\nstrong = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"field"</span>).<span class="fn">filter</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> g: g[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">min</span>() >= <span class="num">80</span>\n)\n<span class="cm"># Only DL remains (min=91), CV (min=76) and NLP (min=79) dropped</span></code></pre></div>'

