/* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: Cross Tabulation & Binning
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">5. Cross Tabulation & Binning</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Cross tabulation is a frequency table for two categorical variables.</strong> While pivot tables aggregate numeric values, <code>pd.crosstab()</code> counts occurrences. It answers questions like "how many students from each field scored in each grade range?"</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"student"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>,<span class="str">"Bob"</span>,<span class="str">"Carol"</span>,<span class="str">"Dave"</span>,<span class="str">"Eve"</span>,<span class="str">"Frank"</span>,<span class="str">"Grace"</span>,<span class="str">"Hank"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>,<span class="str">"CV"</span>,<span class="str">"NLP"</span>,<span class="str">"CV"</span>,<span class="str">"DL"</span>,<span class="str">"NLP"</span>,<span class="str">"DL"</span>,<span class="str">"CV"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"level"</span>: [<span class="str">"grad"</span>,<span class="str">"undergrad"</span>,<span class="str">"grad"</span>,<span class="str">"undergrad"</span>,<span class="str">"grad"</span>,<span class="str">"undergrad"</span>,<span class="str">"grad"</span>,<span class="str">"undergrad"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">92</span>,<span class="num">85</span>,<span class="num">88</span>,<span class="num">76</span>,<span class="num">95</span>,<span class="num">79</span>,<span class="num">91</span>,<span class="num">82</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Basic cross tabulation -- frequency counts</span>\nctab = pd.<span class="fn">crosstab</span>(df[<span class="str">"field"</span>], df[<span class="str">"level"</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(ctab)\n<span class="cm"># level  grad  undergrad</span>\n<span class="cm"># field</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV        0          3</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL        2          0</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP       2          1</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># With margins (totals)</span>\nctab_m = pd.<span class="fn">crosstab</span>(df[<span class="str">"field"</span>], df[<span class="str">"level"</span>], margins=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(ctab_m)\n<span class="cm"># level  grad  undergrad  All</span>\n<span class="cm"># field</span>\n<span class="cm"># CV        0          3    3</span>\n<span class="cm"># DL        2          0    2</span>\n<span class="cm"># NLP       2          1    3</span>\n<span class="cm"># All       4          4    8</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Normalized -- proportions instead of counts</span>\nctab_pct = pd.<span class="fn">crosstab</span>(df[<span class="str">"field"</span>], df[<span class="str">"level"</span>], normalize=<span class="str">"index"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(ctab_pct.<span class="fn">round</span>(<span class="num">2</span>))\n<span class="cm"># normalize="index"  \u2192 row percentages (sum to 1 per row)</span>\n<span class="cm"># normalize="columns" \u2192 column percentages</span>\n<span class="cm"># normalize="all"    \u2192 total percentages</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Binning with pd.cut() and pd.qcut()</strong> -- convert continuous data into categorical groups:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># pd.cut() -- equal-width bins (you define the edges)</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"grade"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">cut</span>(\n    df[<span class="str">"score"</span>],\n    bins=[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">60</span>, <span class="num">70</span>, <span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">90</span>, <span class="num">100</span>],\n    labels=[<span class="str">"F"</span>, <span class="str">"D"</span>, <span class="str">"C"</span>, <span class="str">"B"</span>, <span class="str">"A"</span>]\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[[<span class="str">"student"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"grade"</span>]])\n<span class="cm">#   student  score grade</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0   Alice     92     A</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1     Bob     85     B</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2   Carol     88     B</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3    Dave     76     C</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4     Eve     95     A</span>\n<span class="cm"># 5   Frank     79     C</span>\n<span class="cm"># 6   Grace     91     A</span>\n<span class="cm"># 7    Hank     82     B</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Cross-tab grade vs field</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(pd.<span class="fn">crosstab</span>(df[<span class="str">"grade"</span>], df[<span class="str">"field"</span>]))\n<span class="cm"># field  CV  DL  NLP</span>\n<span class="cm"># grade</span>\n<span class="cm"># A       0   2    1</span>\n<span class="cm"># B       2   0    1</span>\n<span class="cm"># C       1   0    1</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># pd.qcut() -- equal-frequency bins (equal number per bin)</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"quartile"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">qcut</span>(df[<span class="str">"score"</span>], q=<span class="num">4</span>, labels=[<span class="str">"Q1"</span>,<span class="str">"Q2"</span>,<span class="str">"Q3"</span>,<span class="str">"Q4"</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[[<span class="str">"student"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"quartile"</span>]])\n\n<span class="cm"># Feature engineering with groupby + binning</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"score_bin"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">cut</span>(df[<span class="str">"score"</span>], bins=<span class="num">3</span>)  <span class="cm"># auto 3 equal-width bins</span>\nbin_stats = df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"score_bin"</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>(\n    count=(<span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"count"</span>),\n    mean_score=(<span class="str">"score"</span>, <span class="str">"mean"</span>)\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(bin_stats)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">pd.cut() -- Equal-Width Bins</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">You specify bin edges: [0, 60, 80, 100]</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Each bin has the same width</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Different number of items per bin</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Use for: letter grades, age groups, price ranges</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">pd.qcut() -- Equal-Frequency Bins</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">You specify number of quantiles: q=4</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Each bin has the same number of items</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Different bin widths</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Use for: percentile-based groups, ML features</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">PRACTICAL: Feature Engineering with GroupBy</div><div class="example-body">'
+ '<strong>NLP scenario:</strong> You have a dataset of reviews with categories. Feature engineering with groupby:<br>'
+ '<code>df["category_avg_len"] = df.groupby("category")["text_length"].transform("mean")</code> -- average text length per category<br>'
+ '<code>df["len_vs_category"] = df["text_length"] / df["category_avg_len"]</code> -- relative length<br>'
+ '<code>df["category_sentiment_rank"] = df.groupby("category")["sentiment"].rank(pct=True)</code> -- percentile rank within category<br>'
+ '<code>df["is_long_for_category"] = df.groupby("category")["text_length"].transform(lambda x: x > x.quantile(0.9))</code> -- outlier flag'
+ '</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 6: Window Functions
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">6. Window Functions</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Window functions compute statistics over a sliding window of data.</strong> Instead of computing a single value for the whole group, they compute a value for each row based on a neighborhood of surrounding rows. Essential for time series analysis, smoothing, and trend detection.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Rolling mean — average over the last w observations:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\bar{x}_t^{(w)} = \\frac{1}{w}\\sum_{i = t-w+1}^{t} x_i$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x̄_t^{(w)}</div><div class="card-body">Rolling (moving) mean at time t, computed over a fixed window of size w.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">w</div><div class="card-body">Window length. Larger w = smoother but slower to react to changes; smaller w = noisier but more responsive.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Sum range</div><div class="card-body">From t−w+1 to t — the most recent w observations including the current one. The window slides forward one step at a time.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">First w−1 NaN</div><div class="card-body">For the first w−1 timesteps there is not enough history yet, so pandas returns NaN by default.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Equal weights</div><div class="card-body">Each observation in the window contributes 1/w. All points are equally important — no decay. EWM (next formula) fixes this.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Exponentially Weighted Moving Average (EWM) — recursive smoothing with decay:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\bar{x}_t = \\alpha\\, x_t + (1 - \\alpha)\\, \\bar{x}_{t-1}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x̄_t</div><div class="card-body">EWM value at time t. Each new estimate is a weighted blend of the new observation and the previous estimate.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x_t</div><div class="card-body">The fresh observation at time t. Weighted by α.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x̄_{t−1}</div><div class="card-body">The previous EWM value. Already encodes a decaying memory of all earlier observations.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">α (alpha)</div><div class="card-body">Smoothing factor in (0, 1]. α near 1 = react quickly, almost no smoothing; α near 0 = very smooth, slow response. Pandas accepts span s, with α = 2/(s+1).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Why exponential</div><div class="card-body">Expanding the recursion gives weights α·(1−α)^k for the k-th past observation — they shrink geometrically. Old data fades automatically.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Use in ML</div><div class="card-body">Adam/RMSProp optimizers use EWM on gradients; batch norm uses EWM running statistics; financial indicators (EMA) are EWM means.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">rolling()</div><div class="card-body">Fixed-size sliding window. <code>df["col"].rolling(window=7).mean()</code> computes the 7-day moving average. Window slides one row at a time. First <code>window-1</code> values are NaN.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">expanding()</div><div class="card-body">Cumulative window that grows from the start. <code>.expanding().mean()</code> computes the running average. Equivalent to <code>rolling(window=len(df))</code> but more efficient.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">ewm()</div><div class="card-body">Exponentially weighted window. Recent values get more weight. <code>.ewm(span=7).mean()</code> gives recent points exponentially higher influence. Great for smoothing noisy data.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Create time series data</span>\nnp.random.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\ndates = pd.<span class="fn">date_range</span>(<span class="str">"2024-01-01"</span>, periods=<span class="num">90</span>, freq=<span class="str">"D"</span>)\nnoise = np.random.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="num">90</span>) * <span class="num">5</span>\ntrend = np.<span class="fn">linspace</span>(<span class="num">50</span>, <span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">90</span>)\nts = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"date"</span>: dates, <span class="str">"value"</span>: trend + noise})\nts = ts.<span class="fn">set_index</span>(<span class="str">"date"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Rolling mean (moving average)</span>\nts[<span class="str">"ma_7"</span>]  = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(window=<span class="num">7</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()\nts[<span class="str">"ma_21"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(window=<span class="num">21</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># Rolling standard deviation (volatility)</span>\nts[<span class="str">"rolling_std"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(window=<span class="num">7</span>).<span class="fn">std</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># Expanding mean (cumulative average)</span>\nts[<span class="str">"cum_mean"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">expanding</span>().<span class="fn">mean</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># Expanding max (running maximum)</span>\nts[<span class="str">"cum_max"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">expanding</span>().<span class="fn">max</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># EWM (exponentially weighted moving average)</span>\nts[<span class="str">"ewm_7"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">ewm</span>(span=<span class="num">7</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(ts.<span class="fn">tail</span>(<span class="num">10</span>))</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Advanced rolling operations</strong> -- custom functions, min_periods, and centered windows:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># min_periods: minimum data points for a valid result</span>\n<span class="cm"># Default: min_periods=window (all NaN until full window)</span>\nts[<span class="str">"ma_partial"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(window=<span class="num">7</span>, min_periods=<span class="num">1</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()\n<span class="cm"># Now the first value is computed with just 1 data point</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Centered window (looks forward and backward)</span>\nts[<span class="str">"ma_center"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(window=<span class="num">7</span>, center=<span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># Custom rolling function</span>\nts[<span class="str">"rolling_range"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(window=<span class="num">7</span>).<span class="fn">apply</span>(\n    <span class="kw">lambda</span> w: w.<span class="fn">max</span>() - w.<span class="fn">min</span>(),\n    raw=<span class="kw">True</span>   <span class="cm"># raw=True for NumPy array (faster)</span>\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># Multiple rolling aggregations</span>\nrolling_stats = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(window=<span class="num">14</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>(\n    [<span class="str">"mean"</span>, <span class="str">"std"</span>, <span class="str">"min"</span>, <span class="str">"max"</span>]\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># Cumulative statistics</span>\nts[<span class="str">"cumsum"</span>]  = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">cumsum</span>()\nts[<span class="str">"cumprod"</span>] = (ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>] / <span class="num">100</span>).<span class="fn">cumprod</span>()\nts[<span class="str">"cummax"</span>]  = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">cummax</span>()\nts[<span class="str">"cummin"</span>]  = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">cummin</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># Percentage change</span>\nts[<span class="str">"pct_change"</span>] = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">pct_change</span>()       <span class="cm"># day-over-day</span>\nts[<span class="str">"pct_7d"</span>]     = ts[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">pct_change</span>(<span class="num">7</span>)     <span class="cm"># week-over-week</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Rolling Average EN --- */
+ '<div id="plot-rolling-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ 'var n=90;var raw=[];var ma7=[];var ma21=[];var ewm7=[];'
+ 'var rng=function(s){var x=Math.sin(s)*10000;return x-Math.floor(x)};'
+ 'for(var i=0;i<n;i++){var trend=50+i*30/90;var noise=(rng(i+42)-0.5)*10;raw.push(Math.round((trend+noise)*10)/10)}'
+ 'for(var i=0;i<n;i++){if(i<6){ma7.push(null)}else{var s=0;for(var j=i-6;j<=i;j++)s+=raw[j];ma7.push(Math.round(s/7*10)/10)}}'
+ 'for(var i=0;i<n;i++){if(i<20){ma21.push(null)}else{var s=0;for(var j=i-20;j<=i;j++)s+=raw[j];ma21.push(Math.round(s/21*10)/10)}}'
+ 'var alpha=2/(7+1);ewm7[0]=raw[0];for(var i=1;i<n;i++){ewm7.push(Math.round((alpha*raw[i]+(1-alpha)*ewm7[i-1])*10)/10)}'
+ 'var days=[];for(var i=0;i<n;i++){var d=new Date(2024,0,1+i);days.push(d.toISOString().slice(0,10))}'
+ 'var t1={x:days,y:raw,name:"Raw",type:"scatter",mode:"lines",line:{color:"rgba(200,169,110,0.3)",width:1}};'
+ 'var t2={x:days,y:ma7,name:"7-day MA",type:"scatter",mode:"lines",line:{color:"#4de87a",width:2}};'
+ 'var t3={x:days,y:ma21,name:"21-day MA",type:"scatter",mode:"lines",line:{color:"#a78bfa",width:2}};'
+ 'var t4={x:days,y:ewm7,name:"EWM (span=7)",type:"scatter",mode:"lines",line:{color:"#f87171",width:2,dash:"dash"}};'
+ 'var layout={title:{text:"Window Functions: Rolling & Exponential Moving Averages",font:{color:"#ebe6dc",size:14}},paper_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",plot_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",font:{color:"#ebe6dc"},xaxis:{title:"Date",gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.06)"},yaxis:{title:"Value",gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.06)"},legend:{x:0.01,y:0.99,bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0.3)"},margin:{t:50,r:30,b:60,l:50}};'
+ 'Plotly.newPlot("plot-rolling-en",[t1,t2,t3,t4],layout,{responsive:true,displayModeBar:false});'
+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> The faint gold line is the raw noisy data. The green line is the 7-day rolling mean (short-term trend), the purple line is the 21-day rolling mean (long-term trend), and the dashed red line is the exponentially weighted mean (recent-biased smoothing). Longer windows produce smoother curves but react slower to changes.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">PRACTICAL: ML Feature Engineering with Windows</div><div class="example-body">'
+ '<strong>Time series features for ML models:</strong><br>'
+ '<code>df["ma_7"] = df.groupby("category")["value"].transform(lambda x: x.rolling(7).mean())</code> -- per-category moving average<br>'
+ '<code>df["volatility"] = df.groupby("category")["value"].transform(lambda x: x.rolling(14).std())</code> -- rolling volatility<br>'
+ '<code>df["momentum"] = df["value"].pct_change(7)</code> -- 7-period momentum<br>'
+ '<code>df["above_ma"] = (df["value"] > df["ma_7"]).astype(int)</code> -- binary trend indicator<br>'
+ '<code>df["distance_from_max"] = df["value"] / df["value"].expanding().max()</code> -- drawdown from peak'
+ '</div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Performance tip:</strong> Pass <code>raw=True</code> to <code>.apply()</code> in rolling/expanding operations. This gives your function a NumPy array instead of a Series, which is significantly faster. Only use <code>raw=False</code> if you need index information inside the function.</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">GROUPBY CHEAT SHEET</div><div class="example-body">'
+ '<strong>Basics:</strong> <code>df.groupby("col")</code> | <code>df.groupby(["a","b"])</code> | <code>.groups</code> | <code>.ngroups</code> | <code>.get_group("x")</code><br>'
+ '<strong>Aggregation:</strong> <code>.mean()</code> | <code>.sum()</code> | <code>.count()</code> | <code>.agg(["mean","std"])</code> | <code>.agg(name=("col","func"))</code><br>'
+ '<strong>Transform:</strong> <code>.transform("mean")</code> | <code>.transform(lambda x: (x-x.mean())/x.std())</code> | <code>.rank()</code><br>'
+ '<strong>Filter:</strong> <code>.filter(lambda g: g["col"].mean() > threshold)</code><br>'
+ '<strong>Pivot:</strong> <code>pd.pivot_table(df, values, index, columns, aggfunc)</code> | <code>margins=True</code><br>'
+ '<strong>Cross-tab:</strong> <code>pd.crosstab(df["a"], df["b"])</code> | <code>normalize="index"</code><br>'
+ '<strong>Binning:</strong> <code>pd.cut(s, bins)</code> (equal-width) | <code>pd.qcut(s, q)</code> (equal-freq)<br>'
+ '<strong>Window:</strong> <code>.rolling(7).mean()</code> | <code>.expanding().sum()</code> | <code>.ewm(span=7).mean()</code><br>'
+ '<strong>Cumulative:</strong> <code>.cumsum()</code> | <code>.cummax()</code> | <code>.cummin()</code> | <code>.pct_change()</code>'
+ '</div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-warn"><strong>Next Lesson:</strong> Lesson 6 covers <strong>Merging, Joining & Concatenation</strong> -- combining multiple DataFrames with <code>merge()</code>, <code>join()</code>, and <code>concat()</code>. Inner, outer, left, and right joins, multi-key merges, and handling duplicate keys.</div>'


